id: k8s.pod
kind: kubernetes
title: Pod
aliases: pod, smallest deployable unit
tags: workloads, scheduling
questions:
  - what is a Pod and why is it not a container?
invariant: A Pod is one scheduling unit; containers in it share network and can share volumes.
answer_30s: Pods are the smallest schedulable unit; they group one or more containers that must land on the same node and share a network namespace.
answer_2m: |
  The scheduler places a Pod, not a container. Containers in a Pod share the
  network namespace (one IP, one port space) and optionally share volumes.
  Pods are mortal: a Pod that dies is not restarted by the Deployment, it is
  replaced by a new Pod with a new name and IP. That is why Services and not
  Pod IPs are the stable address.
when_to_use:
  - one process per container, tightly coupled helpers only
avoid_when:
  - you want scaling, rolling updates or self-healing (use a controller)
failure_modes:
  - treating a Pod as stable (name and IP change)
  - running unrelated containers in one Pod to share a volume by accident
follow_ups:
  - why does a Pod get a new IP after a restart?
related:
  - k8s.deployment
  - k8s.service
  - k8s.scheduler
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/
